#: src/features/Blog/blogData.tsx:53
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Naively, I assumed the book would give me tools - techniques for decoding "
"people, keys to reading intentions, perhaps even a method for understanding "
"the stranger in my own home. But the book is really anything but.\n"
"\n"
"It argues that our desire to understand others is so strong that we "
"convince ourselves we already do. We take fragments - tone of voice, "
"posture, reputation, context - and from them construct entire "
"personalities. When our guesses happen to be correct, we treat that as "
"proof of our skill. When they fail, we call it an exception.\n"
"\n"
"And so our confidence grows.\n"
"\n"
"But who is a “stranger”? Not just the person in the elevator or the cashier "
"at the store. A stranger can be a colleague you’ve worked with for years. A "
"friend who has shared secrets. Even someone who has quietly entered your "
"everyday life and begun to shape it.\n"
"\n"
"We learn a few details. We hear a few confessions. We observe a few "
"patterns. Then we exhale, believing we have understood. What we rarely "
"notice is how many blanks we have filled in ourselves - motives, "
"intentions, inner states.\n"
"\n"
"We can mistake coherence for truth.\n"
"\n"
"The book contains unsettling examples. One of them centers on a respected "
"figure in the financial world - a man with status, reputation, elite "
"connections, institutional credibility. For decades, investors trusted him. "
"Major players invested. Returns appeared stable. Everything about him "
"signaled reliability.\n"
"\n"
"Behind that facade stood one of the largest financial frauds in history: "
"the scheme of Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"It seems that his operation lasted so long because people felt safe. "
"Reputation became a substitute for verification. Social proof handled "
"skepticism. When millions trust someone, doubt begins to feel almost "
"irrational - even embarrassing.\n"
"\n"
"It becomes easier to assume that someone else has already asked the hard "
"questions."
msgstr ""
"Secara naif, saya mengira buku ini akan memberi saya alat - teknik untuk "
"memecahkan kode orang, kunci untuk membaca niat, dan mungkin bahkan metode "
"untuk memahami orang asing di rumah saya sendiri. Tetapi buku ini "
"sebenarnya sama sekali bukan.\n"
"\n"
"Buku ini berpendapat bahwa keinginan kita untuk memahami orang lain sangat "
"kuat sehingga kita meyakinkan diri sendiri bahwa kita sudah melakukannya. "
"Kita mengambil fragmen - nada suara, postur tubuh, reputasi, konteks - dan "
"dari situ kita membangun kepribadian secara keseluruhan. Ketika tebakan "
"kita kebetulan benar, kita menganggap itu sebagai bukti keahlian kita. "
"Ketika gagal, kita menyebutnya pengecualian.\n"
"\n"
"Dan dengan demikian, kepercayaan diri kita tumbuh.\n"
"\n"
"Tetapi siapa itu \"orang asing\"? Tidak hanya orang dalam lift atau kasir "
"di toko. Seorang asing dapat menjadi rekan kerja yang sudah Anda kenal "
"selama bertahun-tahun. Seorang teman yang telah berbagi rahasia. Bahkan "
"seseorang yang diam-diam masuk dalam kehidupan sehari-hari Anda dan mulai "
"membentuknya.\n"
"\n"
"Kita mempelajari beberapa rincian. Kita mendengar beberapa pengakuan. Kita "
"mengamati beberapa pola. Lalu kita menghela napas, percaya bahwa kita telah "
"memahami. Yang jarang kita sadari adalah berapa banyak kekosongan yang "
"telah kita isi sendiri - motif, niat, keadaan batin.\n"
"\n"
"Kita bisa keliru menganggap koherensi sebagai kebenaran.\n"
"\n"
"Buku ini berisi contoh-contoh yang menggelisahkan. Salah satunya berpusat "
"pada sosok terhormat di dunia keuangan - seorang pria dengan status, "
"reputasi, koneksi elit, kredibilitas institusi. Selama beberapa dekade, "
"investor mempercayainya. Pemain besar berinvestasi. Pengembalian terlihat "
"stabil. Segala sesuatu tentang dirinya mengisyaratkan keandalan.\n"
"\n"
"Di balik fasad itu berdiri salah satu penipuan keuangan terbesar dalam "
"sejarah: skema Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"Tampaknya operasi dia berlangsung begitu lama karena orang merasa aman. "
"Reputasi menjadi pengganti verifikasi. Bukti sosial menangani skeptisisme. "
"Ketika jutaan orang mempercayai seseorang, keraguan mulai terasa hampir "
"irasional - bahkan memalukan.\n"
"\n"
"Menjadi lebih mudah untuk berasumsi bahwa orang lain sudah mengajukan "
"pertanyaan sulit itu."

